WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 175: Franklin D. Roosevelt: The Arsenal of Democracy and Pearl Harbor (1939–1941)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.840
[Rebecca] Today, we enter the crucible of the 20th century with the second part of our Franklin D.

4
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.360
[Rebecca] Roosevelt saga.

5
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:27.280
[Rebecca] In 1940, with Britain standing alone against Adolf Hitler's seemingly invincible war machine, Roosevelt transformed the United States from an isolationist bystander into the industrial powerhouse of freedom.

6
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:37.600
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Lendleis, the rendezvous in Newfoundland, and the morning of infamy that changed history forever.

7
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:46.480
[George] In the spring of 1940, Adolf Hitler's armoured panzer divisions smashed across the Low Countries and shattered France in six stunning weeks.

8
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:54.720
[George] The British army was miraculously evacuated from the beaches of Dunkirk, leaving behind all heavy artillery, tanks, and transport.

9
00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:06.400
[George] As Winston Churchill assumed the prime ministership and promised only blood, toil, tears, and sweat, Great Britain stood entirely alone against the terrifying might of Nazi Germany.

10
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:16.160
[George] Across the Atlantic, the American people watched the horror with profound dread, yet 80% of the public adamantly opposed entering another European war.

11
00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:32.240
[George] The America First Committee, led by national aviation hero Charles Lindbergh, attracted hundreds of thousands of passionate members who argued that European democracy was already doomed and that the United States must fortify its own borders and let the old world bleed.

12
00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.160
[George] Roosevelt understood what the isolationists refused to see.

13
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:45.920
[George] If Great Britain fell, the entire Atlantic Ocean would become an open highway for Axis conquest, leaving the Western Hemisphere encircled and defenseless.

14
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:54.240
[George] Operating under severe legal constraints from the Neutrality Acts, Roosevelt initiated the bold destroyers for Basies Agreement.

15
00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:08.360
[George] Trading 50 obsolescent World War I-American destroyers to Britain in exchange for 99-year leases on vital British naval bases from Newfoundland to the Caribbean, daringly testing the outer boundaries of executive authority.

16
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:19.480
[George] Roosevelt understood that Hitler's ambition was not confined to Europe, but represented a total ideological assault upon Western civilization that would eventually reach American shores.

17
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.520
[George] No American president had ever dared to seek a third consecutive term in office.

18
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:33.000
[George] Since George Washington stepped down in 1796, the two-term tradition had possessed the sanctity of unwritten constitutional law.

19
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:41.880
[George] But as the international catastrophe deepened, Roosevelt concluded that changing national leadership in the midst of global peril would invite disaster.

20
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:54.440
[George] At the Democratic National Convention in Chicago in July 1940, Roosevelt orchestrated a dramatic draft from behind the scenes, allowing the convention superintendent to announce over loudspeakers.

21
00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.040
[George] We want Roosevelt.

22
00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.120
[George] The world wants Roosevelt.

23
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:06.680
[George] Winning the nomination on the first ballot, FDR faced a formidable Republican challenger, Wendell Wilkie, a charismatic.

24
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:15.320
[George] Dynamic Indiana utilities executive who supported aid to Britain but charged Roosevelt with warmongering and aspiring to dictatorship.

25
00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:23.240
[George] In the closing weeks of the campaign, as Wilkie surged in the polls, Roosevelt delivered a pledge in Boston that would haunt his later months.

26
00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:27.720
[George] I have said this before, but I shall say it again and again and again.

27
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:40.720
[George] On November 5, 1940, the American people chose continuity, re-electing Roosevelt with 27 million votes to Willkie's 22 million.

28
00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:50.960
[George] The election of 1940 proved to the embattled democracies of the world that the American people remained firmly committed to steady leadership in the face of totalitarian aggression.

29
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:58.320
[George] In December 1940, Winston Churchill dispatched a desperate, 4,000-word letter to the White House.

30
00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:08.640
[George] Britain was virtually bankrupt, its gold reserves were exhausted, shipping losses to U-boats were catastrophic, and it could no longer pay cash for American munitions.

31
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:20.480
[George] Taking a 10-day Caribbean cruise aboard the USS Tuscaloosa to ponder the crisis, Roosevelt conceived the most ingenious legislative concept of his presidency, Lendleis.

32
00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:28.000
[George] Returning to Washington, he explained the revolutionary idea to reporters using a homely, unforgettable metaphor.

33
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.880
[George] Suppose my neighbor's home catches fire, and I have a length of garden hose.

34
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.240
[George] If he can take that hose and connect it up with his hydrant, I may help him to put out his fire.

35
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.160
[George] Now, what do I do?

36
00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:47.280
[George] I don't say to him, Neighbor, my garden hose cost me $15, you have to pay me $15 for it.

37
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:48.080
[George] No.

38
00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.680
[George] I want my garden hose back after the fire is over.

39
00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:59.760
[George] On December 29, in a historic fireside chat, Roosevelt summoned the nation to unprecedented industrial mobilization.

40
00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.560
[George] We must be the great arsenal of democracy.

41
00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.400
[George] For us, this is an emergency as serious as war itself.

42
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:20.480
[George] Enacted as House Resolution 1776, Lendleis eliminated the dollar sign from foreign aid, authorizing $50 billion in food, tanks and aircraft that sustained Britain, and later the Soviet Union.

43
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:33.960
[George] Through the darkest nights of the war, by eliminating financial debt barriers from Allied military procurement, Lendleis turned American factories into the decisive engine of victory long before American troops fired a shot.

44
00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:43.160
[George] In August 1941, Roosevelt boarded the presidential yacht Potomac for an ostensibly leisurely fishing trip along the New England coast.

45
00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:55.480
[George] Under cover of darkness and radio silence, the president transferred to the heavy cruiser USS Augusta and sailed north to the foggy, sheltered waters of Placentia Bay off the coast of Newfoundland.

46
00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:06.760
[George] On the morning of August 9, the majestic British battleship HMS Prince of Wales steamed into the bay, bearing Winston Churchill for their first face-to-face meeting as heads of government.

47
00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:12.920
[George] The two Titans formed an instant, profound personal bond that would anchor the Allied war effort.

48
00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:29.080
[George] On Sunday morning, British and American sailors gathered together on the quarterdeck of the Prince of Wales, singing onward, Christian soldiers and O God, our help in ages past under the ship's massive 14-inch guns, with tears streaming down Churchill's cheeks.

49
00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:46.280
[George] Out of this secret summit emerged the Atlantic Charter, a magnificent joint declaration of eight fundamental democratic principles, no territorial aggrandizement, the right of all peoples to choose their own form of government, freedom of the seas, and global economic cooperation.

50
00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:57.000
[George] Though America was still officially neutral, Roosevelt and Churchill had drawn the moral blueprint for the postwar world and pledged their nations to the total destruction of Nazi tyranny.

51
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:08.440
[George] The Atlantic Charter established the enduring moral foundation for the postwar international order, asserting that all sovereign nations possess an inherent right to self-determination and peace.

52
00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:17.280
[George] While Roosevelt's strategic eyes were focused primarily on the Nazi threat in Europe, an equally dangerous crisis was spiraling toward war in Asia.

53
00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:32.640
[George] Japan's militarist regime, bogged down in an eight-year war of conquest in China, sought to seize the oil, rubber, and mineral riches of British Malaya and the Dutch East Indies to establish its Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere.

54
00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:41.200
[George] When Japanese forces occupied southern French Indochina in July 1941, Roosevelt took a devastating economic countermeasure.

55
00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:48.240
[George] He froze all Japanese assets in the United States and imposed a total embargo on petroleum exports.

56
00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.160
[George] Japan imported 80% of its oil from the United States.

57
00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:58.080
[George] Without American fuel, the Imperial Japanese Navy would run dry within 18 months.

58
00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:05.520
[George] Militarists in Tokyo, led by General Haidki Tojo, decided that war with the United States was inevitable.

59
00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:15.440
[George] Through codebreaking intercepts codenamed Magic, American military intelligence knew that a Japanese strike was imminent somewhere in the Pacific by early December.

60
00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:23.120
[George] But military commanders in Washington assumed the strike would target the Philippines, Malaya, or the Dutch East Indies.

61
00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:34.720
[George] Never dreaming that Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto's six carrier strike force was slicing silently across 3,000 miles of northern Pacific swells directly toward the Hawaiian Islands.

62
00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:42.320
[George] The tragic miscalculation of Japanese militarists was their belief that a sudden devastating blow would demoralize America.

63
00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:46.800
[George] Instead, it ignited an unquenchable furnace of national resolve.

64
00:08:46.800 --> 00:09:00.000
[George] At 7 55 on Sunday morning, December 7, 1941, the peaceful tropical tranquility of Oahu was shattered by the scream of dive bombers and the roar of torpedoes bearing the rising sun insignia.

65
00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:08.360
[George] 353 Japanese aircraft swarmed over Battleship Row in Pearl Harbor and military airfields across the island.

66
00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:14.120
[George] In less than two harrowing hours, 18 American warships were sunk or heavily damaged.

67
00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:26.680
[George] The battleship USS Arizona exploded in a colossal fireball when an armor-piercing bomb pierced its forward magazine, entombing 1,177 young sailors beneath the oil-slicked waters.

68
00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:34.600
[George] Over 300 American aircraft were destroyed on the ground, and 2,403 Americans lost their lives.

69
00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:44.840
[George] In the White House, Roosevelt was in his second floor study sorting his beloved stamp collection when Secretary of the Navy Frank Knox phoned at 1.40 in the afternoon.

70
00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:47.800
[George] Knox was crying as he stammered, Mr.

71
00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:52.280
[George] President, it looks like the Japanese have attacked Pearl Harbor.

72
00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:56.920
[George] Roosevelt's face turned white with shock, but his voice was ice-cold.

73
00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:58.920
[George] It can't be true, Frank.

74
00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.200
[George] But if it is, it takes the matter out of our hands.

75
00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:08.840
[George] That night, Roosevelt met with his somber cabinet and dictated a short, immortal address to Congress.

76
00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:19.560
[George] The catastrophic losses at Pearl Harbor forged a grim determination across the United States, transforming millions of ordinary citizens into an invincible fighting force.

77
00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:23.400
[George] On December 8, 1941, Franklin D.

78
00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:27.720
[George] Roosevelt was wheeled down the ramp into the well of the House of Representatives.

79
00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:38.440
[George] Gripping the arm of his son James, he pulled himself erect on his locked steel leg braces before a joint session of Congress and a radio audience of 60 million stunned citizens.

80
00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:42.840
[George] His opening words became carved forever into the granite of human memory.

81
00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:48.800
[George] Yesterday, December 7th, 1941, a date which will live in infamy.

82
00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:55.600
[George] The United States of America was suddenly and deliberately attacked by naval and air forces of the Empire of Japan.

83
00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:03.120
[George] In seven electric minutes, Roosevelt asked Congress for a declaration of war, declaring with unyielding conviction.

84
00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:11.760
[George] No matter how long it may take us to overcome this premeditated invasion, the American people in their righteous might will win through to absolute victory.

85
00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:15.280
[George] The House voted 388 to 1 for war.

86
00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:17.200
[George] The Senate was unanimous.

87
00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:25.200
[George] Three days later, Adolf Hitler and Benito Mussolini made the fatal strategic blunder of declaring war on the United States.

88
00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:31.200
[George] In a single week, all political division, isolationism, and hesitation evaporated.

89
00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:41.360
[George] The sleeping American colossus was fully awake, and Franklin Roosevelt took the helm as commander-in-chief of the greatest crusade for human liberty the world had ever known.

90
00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:52.960
[George] In mobilizing the nation for total global conflict, Franklin Roosevelt rallied a free people to defend democracy with an industrial and military might never before seen on Earth.

91
00:11:53.360 --> 00:12:00.960
[Rebecca] The attack on Pearl Harbor plunged the United States into global conflict, uniting a divided nation behind Franklin D.

92
00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:03.280
[Rebecca] Roosevelt's unflinching leadership.

93
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:15.680
[Rebecca] But the road from catastrophe to victory would demand total industrial mobilization, unprecedented military cooperation across three continents, and agonizing human sacrifice.

94
00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:18.000
[Rebecca] Next week, we conclude our Franklin D.

95
00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:21.600
[Rebecca] Roosevelt trilogy with his role as Supreme Allied Commander.

96
00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:34.360
[Rebecca] The Casablanca Declaration of Unconditional Surrender, the epic invasion of Normandy on D Day, the crucial negotiations with Churchill and Stalin at Yalta, and his sudden death on the eve of final victory.

97
00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:39.320
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

